In .NET 10 (targeting Android 15+), `adjustResize` is effectively deprecated because edge-to-edge rendering is enforced. The fix is to react to **keyboard (IME) insets** instead.

**Recommended approach — handle insets in your `MainActivity`:**

```csharp
// Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;

protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window!.DecorView, new InsetsListener());
}

private class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

        // Apply bottom padding = max of keyboard or nav bar
        v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom));

        return insets;
    }
}
```

**Also ensure** in `Platforms/Android/AndroidManifest.xml`:
```xml
<activity android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" ... />
```

Even though `adjustResize` alone no longer resizes content, it's still needed so the system dispatches IME insets to your view tree.

**Key points:**
- `WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime()` gives you the keyboard height
- The listener fires when the keyboard opens/closes, letting you pad the content up
- This replaces the old one-liner and works with Android 15's edge-to-edge model

If you want a pure XAML approach, you can also bind your input bar's `Margin` to a value updated from a platform-specific service that observes these insets.
